Project: Marble game

Physics:
RigidBodies: The player and moving obstacles use Rigidbody components to enable physics-based movement and interaction with the environment. 
Force Application: Player movement is implemented using torque-based force. Instead of setting velocity, torque is applied to simulate a rolling marble.
Colliders: The player and other objects use colliders to detect and respond to physical interactions. For example, the player uses sphere collider for smooth rolling. Obstacles and terrain use Box Colliders to detect when they are touched by the player.
Physics Materials: We use friction material for the player to allow smooth rolling. We also use the bounce material for some obstacles to make the player bounce from the obstacle.
Collision/Trigger Events: We use OnTriggerEnter() for collectibles and hazards, allowing detection. OnColiisionEnter() is used for obstacle impacts.

Reflection:

Wendy: I contributed to the creation of some maps, and implementing the spikes, bouncy obstacles, and coin collectibles. While working on these features, I learned to use tools such as Spline and ProBuilder. I used ProBuilder to create cones for the spikes since Unity does not include that shape by default. Therefore, I had to install ProBuilder to have more variety of options for objects/shapes. I also learned Spline to create curved roads to make the game more visually appealing and dynamic. With that tool, I also learned that I could import examples to be able to use for the game. With that, I was able to impplement the road. Overall, this process helped me learn how to use tools that can be useful for creating better games.
